**Sec. 10101.** This section redefines the Thrifty Food Plan to use the 2021 USDA report as the baseline, establishing fixed household size ratios (e.g., 30% for one person, 100% for four) for SNAP allotments. It mandates annual cost adjustments based on the Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers starting October 1, 2025, and allows for a one-time re-evaluation of market baskets no earlier than October 1, 2027, provided the re-evaluation does not increase the plan's cost.

**Sec. 10102.** This section modifies SNAP work requirements for able-bodied adults by expanding exemptions to include pregnant women, individuals under 18 or over 65, and specific Indian populations. It standardizes enforcement by defining "noncontiguous states" as excluding Guam and the Virgin Islands and allows the Secretary to grant temporary exemptions to noncontiguous states demonstrating good faith efforts to comply, with exemptions expiring no later than December 31, 2028.

**Sec. 10103.** This section amends SNAP utility allowance rules to ensure that households with an elderly or disabled member are eligible for standard utility allowances. It clarifies that third-party energy assistance payments are treated differently for households with versus without an elderly or disabled member when calculating excess shelter expenses.

**Sec. 10104.** This section prohibits the inclusion of any service fees associated with internet connections in the calculation of the excess shelter expense deduction for SNAP benefits.

**Sec. 10105.** This section introduces a state quality control incentive starting in fiscal year 2028, where the federal share of SNAP costs varies based on the state's payment error rate, ranging from 100% federal share for error rates under 6% to 85% federal share for rates of 10% or higher. It allows states to use 2025 or 2026 error rates for the 2028 calculation and delays implementation for states with high error rates in 2025 or 2026.

**Sec. 10106.** This section reduces the federal administrative cost share for SNAP from 50% to 25% for fiscal year 2027 and each subsequent fiscal year, while maintaining the 50% share through fiscal year 2026.

**Sec. 10107.** This section limits the funding for the National Education and Obesity Prevention Grant Program to fiscal years 2016 through 2025, effectively ending the mandatory funding authorization after that period.

**Sec. 10108.** This section restricts SNAP eligibility to U.S. residents who are citizens, nationals, lawfully admitted permanent residents, Cuban and Haitian entrants, or individuals residing under a Compact of Free Association. It explicitly excludes temporary visitors, tourists, diplomats, and students from eligibility and requires that the income and resources of ineligible aliens be counted in the household's benefit calculation.

**Sec. 10201.** This section rescinds unobligated balances of funds appropriated for forestry programs in Public Law 117-169, specifically targeting amounts in sections 23001, 23002, 23003, and 23005.

**Sec. 10301.** This section increases the effective reference price percentage from 85% to 88% starting with the 2025 crop year and establishes fixed reference prices for covered commodities (e.g., $6.35/bushel for wheat, $4.10/bushel for corn) for the 2025 crop year. It mandates that reference prices increase by 0.5% annually starting in 2031, capped at 113% of the 2025 baseline prices.

**Sec. 10302.** This section authorizes the Secretary to allocate up to 30,000,000 additional base acres to eligible farms starting in the 2026 crop year, based on a 5-year average of planted acreage from 2019 through 2023. It extends the base acreage election period to 2031 and establishes payment yields for these new acres based on existing farm yields or county averages.

**Sec. 10303.** This section extends the producer election period for crop insurance and commodity programs to 2031 and allows producers to elect the 2026 crop year for certain coverage determinations. It mandates that for the 2025 crop year, producers receive the higher of Price Loss Coverage or Agriculture Risk Coverage payments on a commodity-by-commodity basis.

**Sec. 10304.** This section extends the Price Loss Coverage program through the 2031 crop year and updates the calculation of the average market price to use data from 2017 through 2021 instead of 2012 through 2016.

**Sec. 10305.** This section extends the Agriculture Risk Coverage program through the 2031 crop year and adjusts the benchmark revenue calculation to include 100% of revenue for 2014–2024 and 90% for 2025–2031. It increases the disaster payment threshold from 10% to 12% of benchmark revenue for crop years 2025 through 2031.

**Sec. 10306.** This section expands the definition of entities eligible for equitable treatment in farm programs to include "qualified pass-through entities," such as partnerships, S corporations, LLCs, and joint ventures. It updates payment attribution and liability rules to apply to these entities rather than just joint ventures and general partnerships.

**Sec. 10307.** This section increases the annual payment limitation for commodity programs from $125,000 to $155,000 and mandates annual inflation adjustments to this limit starting in the 2025 crop year based on the Consumer Price Index.

**Sec. 10308.** This section creates an exception to the Adjusted Gross Income limitation for individuals or entities receiving certain excepted payments (such as crop insurance or disaster assistance) if at least 75% of their average gross income derives from farming, ranching, or silviculture activities.

**Sec. 10309.** This section extends nonrecourse marketing assistance loans through the 2031 crop year and sets specific loan rates for 2026–2031 (e.g., $3.72/bushel for wheat, $2.42/bushel for corn). It authorizes cotton storage payments for 2026–2031 at rates up to $4.90 in California/Arizona and $3.00 elsewhere, and extends loan deficiency payments and recourse loans through 2031.

**Sec. 10310.** This section modifies marketing loan repayment rules to allow repayment at the lesser of the loan rate or the prevailing world market price for rice and upland cotton. It introduces a refund mechanism for upland cotton producers if the world market price drops within 30 days of repayment and establishes specific price determination formulas for extra long staple cotton.

**Sec. 10311.** This section increases the economic adjustment assistance rate for textile mills from 3 cents per pound to 5 cents per pound for periods beginning on or after August 1, 2025.

**Sec. 10312.** This section sets the raw cane sugar loan rate at 24.00 cents per pound for 2025–2031 and the refined sugar loan rate at 136.55% of the raw rate. It mandates minimum CCC storage rates for forfeited sugar (34 cents/hundredweight for refined, 27 cents for raw) and requires the Secretary to reallocate tariff-rate quota shortfalls and conduct a study on refined sugar import terms.

**Sec. 10313.** This section changes the Dairy Margin Coverage production history to the highest annual milk marketings from 2021, 2022, or 2023, and increases the maximum production history cap from 5,000,000 to 6,000,000 pounds. It extends the program through 2031 and updates premium discount calculations to use data from 2026 through 2031.

**Sec. 10314.** This section appropriates $50,000,000 to fund the implementation of the commodity program changes in this bill, with specific allocations for dairy surveys ($9,000,000), sugar studies ($1,000,000), and other administrative activities.

**Sec. 10401.** This section increases Livestock Indemnity Program payment rates to 100% of market value for predation losses and 75% for adverse weather or disease losses, and adds a new payment for unborn livestock death losses at up to 85% of the lowest weight class rate. It modifies the Livestock Forage Disaster Program to allow two monthly payments for shorter grazing periods and adds emergency assistance for farm-raised fish losses due to bird depredation at a minimum rate of $600 per acre.

**Sec. 10501.** This section extends the definition of a beginning farmer or rancher from 5 to 10 years of experience and provides additional premium assistance of 5 percentage points for the first two reinsurance years, 3 points for the third, and 1 point for the fourth year.

**Sec. 10502.** This section increases the maximum coverage levels for area-based crop insurance to 95% and individual coverage to 85% (or 90% for aggregated commodities), and raises the premium subsidy for area plans from 65% to 80%.

**Sec. 10503.** This section requires the Risk Management Agency to pay an additional 6% administrative and operating expense subsidy for contracts in states with loss ratios exceeding 120% of net book premium starting in 2026. It mandates minimum reimbursement rates for specialty crops and requires annual inflation adjustments to administrative and operating expense reimbursements.

**Sec. 10504.** This section increases federal premium subsidies for various crop insurance plans by 5 percentage points, raising the base subsidy for most plans from 64% to 69% and adjusting other tiers accordingly.

**Sec. 10505.** This section increases the annual funding for crop insurance program compliance and integrity activities from $4,000,000 to $6,000,000 for fiscal year 2026 and each subsequent fiscal year.

**Sec. 10506.** This section increases the annual funding for crop insurance reviews, compliance, and integrity activities to $10,000,000 for fiscal year 2026 and each fiscal year thereafter.

**Sec. 10507.** This section establishes a pilot program for index-based poultry insurance covering extreme weather-related utility costs, requiring the Risk Management Agency to engage stakeholders and approve a policy or plan within two years of enactment.

**Sec. 10601.** This section increases funding for conservation programs, including the Conservation Reserve Program ($625M–$700M annually), Environmental Quality Incentives Program ($2.655B–$3.255B annually), and Regional Conservation Partnership Program ($425M–$450M annually) for fiscal years 2026 through 2031. It also increases funding for watershed protection to $150,000,000 annually and adds $105,000,000 for feral swine control over the same period.

**Sec. 10602.** This section establishes a Supplemental Agricultural Trade Promotion Program with mandatory funding of $285,000,000 from the Commodity Credit Corporation for fiscal year 2027 and each fiscal year thereafter to expand export markets.

**Sec. 10603.** This section extends the Emergency Food Assistance Program funding authorization through fiscal year 2031.

**Sec. 10604.** This section extends funding for urban and indoor agricultural research through 2031, transfers $37,000,000 to the Foundation for Food and Agriculture Research, and appropriates $60,000,000 for scholarships at 1890 institutions for fiscal year 2026. It also mandates $8,000,000 for assistive technology for farmers with disabilities and increases Specialty Crop Research Initiative funding to $175,000,000 for fiscal year 2026.

**Sec. 10605.** This section extends the Rural Energy for America Program funding authorization through fiscal year 2031.

**Sec. 10606.** This section increases funding for plant pest and disease management to $90,000,000 for fiscal year 2026 and specialty crop block grants to $100,000,000 for fiscal year 2026. It also extends the National Organic Certification Cost-share Program through 2031 and adds $5,000,000 for the Multiple Crop and Pesticide Use Survey for fiscal year 2026.

**Sec. 10607.** This section increases funding for animal disease prevention and management to $233,000,000 annually for fiscal years 2026–2030 and $75,000,000 for 2031. It extends the Pima Cotton Trust Fund, Wool Apparel Manufacturers Trust Fund, and Emergency Citrus Disease Research Trust Fund through 2031 and adds $3,000,000 for the Sheep Production and Marketing Grant Program for fiscal year 2026. [end of text]


